build/
.gradle/
target/
*.class
.idea/
*.iml
.DS_Store
__pycache__/
_to_delete/

!gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar

# Agent-isolated git worktrees are created here, inside the repo (issue #30).
.claude/

# The graph, and the affinity data ADR 33 keeps out of version control (issue #37).
# The default path is ${user.home}/.segue/segue.db, outside the tree — but SEGUE_DB can
# point anywhere, including here.
*.db
*.db-journal
*.db-wal

# The bulk seeding tool's input and output (issue #49, ADR 40). A list of who someone listens to,
# reads and watches IS the personal data ADR 33 governs, and so is the name-to-QID mapping derived
# from it. Both belong outside the working tree; this line is the second lock, not the first.
# No exception is currently needed — if a committed CSV ever is, name it here with an explicit
# negation and say in the commit message why it carries no personal data.
*.csv

# The graph exporter's output (issue #50, ADR 41). A world-fact export carries no personal data
# (ADR 33) and would still be a large derived artefact with no business in git; run with
# --include-affinity and it IS personal data, exactly like the seeding tool's files above.
# Write these outside the working tree; this line is the second lock, not the first.
*.dot
*.graphml

# The ratings tool's output (issue #69, ADR 43). This is the worst of the three: the seeding
# tool's files are a list of names and an affinity export is a picture with ratings on it, but
# this file IS the taste layer — every rating, every note, in one place, and the one part of
# segue that cannot be regenerated from a source. Write it outside the working tree; this line
# and the header the tool writes into the file itself are the second and third locks, not the
# first. No committed .txt is currently needed — if one ever is, name it here with an explicit
# negation and say in the commit message why it carries no personal data.
*.txt

# Agent scratch, and the reason it is named here rather than left merely untracked
# (issue #104 review). The subagent-driven workflow writes task reports and measurements
# into .superpowers/, and several of issue #104's held the owner's rated entities by name —
# the taste layer ADR 33 governs, in the same repository that is public. They stayed out of
# commits only because staging was done by explicit path every time. One `git add -A`, by a
# human or an agent, would have swept them in. This line makes that discipline a property of
# the repository instead of a habit.
.superpowers/

# Contact cards. A vCard is somebody's name, phone number and address — other people's
# personal data, which ADR 33's reasoning covers even though it is not the taste layer.
# Same second-lock argument as *.csv and *.txt above.
*.vcf
